const e={zh:"失传古菜的消亡原因与文化变迁考察",en:"Why Ancient Dishes Vanished: A Study of Culinary Loss and Cultural Change"},a={zh:"本笔记基于本站复刻菜品的文献梳理与厨房实践，尝试回答一个问题：这些曾经真实存在于中国餐桌上的菜，为什么消失了？",en:"Based on the textual research and kitchen experiments behind the dishes archived on this site, this essay tries to answer one question: why did these dishes — once real presences on Chinese tables — disappear?"},n=[{heading:{zh:"引言：为什么它们消失了？",en:"Introduction: Why Did They Disappear?"},paras:[{zh:"中国古代饮食文化灿烂辉煌，仅宋代文献中留有记载的菜肴就数以千计。然而，当我们试图在今天的餐桌上寻找唐宋元明清的古老滋味时，却发现绝大多数菜品已经杳无踪迹。生活史作家徐鲤、卢冉在整理《山家清供》《中馈录》等宋元典籍时发现，其中记载的绝大多数菜品“现已失传”，需要亲手复制、烹饪才能重现。",en:"Ancient Chinese food culture was brilliant and vast. In Song dynasty texts alone, thousands of dishes are recorded. Yet when we try to find the ancient flavors of the Tang, Song, Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties on today’s dining tables, we discover that the vast majority have vanished without a trace. When food historians Xu Li and Lu Ran compiled Shanjia Qinggong, Zhongkui Lu, and other Song-Yuan texts, they found that most of the dishes recorded there “have now been lost” and must be recreated by hand to be experienced again."},{zh:"本文以我复刻的古菜为样本——涵盖唐、宋、元、明、清五个朝代，涉及鱼、蟹、羊肉、豆腐、米面、蔬菜六个种类——系统梳理这些古菜失传的原因，并从中国历史发展与文化变迁的宏观视角加以阐释。",en:"This article takes the dishes I have recreated as samples — spanning the Tang, Song, Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties, and covering six categories: fish, crab, lamb, tofu, rice/noodles, and vegetables — to systematically analyze why these ancient dishes were lost, and to explain this from the broader perspective of Chinese historical development and cultural transformation."}]},{heading:{zh:"第一章 文献的散佚：失传的第一道关口",en:"Chapter One: The Scattering of Texts — The First Barrier to Survival"},paras:[{zh:"在讨论任何具体菜品的消亡之前，我们必须先面对一个基础性问题：烹饪知识本身的传承载体极其脆弱。",en:"Before discussing the disappearance of any specific dish, we must first confront a fundamental problem: the carriers of culinary knowledge are extremely fragile."},{zh:"中国古代的烹饪著作数量庞大，但绝大多数已经亡佚。北魏政治家崔浩撰有《食经》九卷，记录其母亲卢氏及家族女性长辈的烹饪经验，但在唐代以后便已散佚，如今只能从《齐民要术》的引文中窥知一二。魏晋南北朝时期，饮食类著述达三四十种，形成一个高峰，“可惜这些著述绝大部分都已佚失”。南北朝时期的《食珍录》同样原书已佚，仅存二百余字辑录于明代《说郛》中。唐代《食医心鉴》在靖康之难后“罕见，甚至有已亡佚的可能，至明代原书彻底在国内亡佚”。",en:"Ancient China produced a vast number of culinary works, but the vast majority have been lost. The Northern Wei politician Cui Hao wrote a Shijing (Food Classic) in nine volumes, recording the culinary experience of his mother, Lady Lu, and other female elders of the family. But it was lost after the Tang dynasty, and today we can only glimpse fragments quoted in Qimin Yaoshu. During the Wei-Jin and Northern-Southern dynasties, there were thirty or forty works on food, forming a peak — “unfortunately, the vast majority of these works have been lost.” The Shizhen Lu from the Northern-Southern period is likewise lost; only about two hundred characters survive, recorded in the Ming dynasty Shuofu. The Tang dynasty Shiyi Xinjian became “rare, and possibly lost” after the Jingkang Incident, and by the Ming dynasty the original was completely lost within China."},{zh:"这意味着什么？意味着大量的古代菜品，我们只知其名，不知其法。以宋代为例，“现存文献中载有具体做法的食物最多只剩百余种”。而宋代饮食之繁盛，仅《东京梦华录》中提到的菜肴名称就有数百种之多。绝大多数菜品在文献记录的环节就已经丢失了。",en:"What does this mean? It means that for a great many ancient dishes, we know only the name, not the method. In the Song dynasty, for example, “only about a hundred or so foods survive in extant texts with specific preparation methods.” Yet Song dynasty cuisine was so flourishing that Dongjing Menghua Lu alone mentions hundreds of dish names. The vast majority of dishes were already lost at the stage of written record."},{zh:"烹饪文献散佚的原因是多重的：",en:"The reasons for the scattering of culinary texts are multiple:"}],subs:[{subheading:{zh:"其一，战乱与灾荒对典籍的毁灭。",en:"First, war and famine destroyed books."},paras:[{zh:"靖康之难、宋末元初的战乱、明清易代的动荡，每一次大规模社会动乱都伴随着典籍的大量焚毁与散失。《食医心鉴》的亡佚就与靖康之难直接相关。",en:"The Jingkang Incident, the wars at the end of the Song and beginning of the Yuan, the turmoil of the Ming-Qing transition — each large-scale social upheaval was accompanied by the mass burning and loss of texts. The loss of Shiyi Xinjian is directly related to the Jingkang Incident."}]},{subheading:{zh:"其二，政治迫害导致的禁书。",en:"Second, political persecution led to banned books."},paras:[{zh:"北魏崔浩因“国书事件”被彻底抹黑，他撰写的《食经》也被列为禁书，加速了这部重要饮食文献的消亡。",en:"Because of the “National History Incident,” Cui Hao was thoroughly discredited, and his Shijing was also listed as a banned book, accelerating the disappearance of this important culinary text."}]},{subheading:{zh:"其三，饮食知识的“小道”地位。",en:"Third, culinary knowledge held “minor” status."},paras:[{zh:"在中国传统学术体系中，饮食烹饪属于“小道”，不被正统学术界重视。许多文人虽记录食谱，但往往只是“姑以寄意而已不足以资考核也”。这种轻视使得饮食文献在传承中缺乏系统性的整理和保护。",en:"In the traditional Chinese academic system, cooking and food were considered “minor matters” and were not taken seriously by orthodox academia. Many literati recorded recipes, but often only “to express a passing interest, not sufficient for serious examination.” This disdain meant that culinary texts lacked systematic preservation and protection."},{zh:"这个背景至关重要：这些菜中有相当一部分之所以失传，首先是因为连“方子”都没有传下来。乳酿鱼在烧尾宴食单中仅有菜名，没有详细做法；毛厨三鲜汤的“三鲜”具体指哪三种食材，因缺乏记载而难以确定。文献的缺失使得复刻工作从一开始就面临“无据可依”的困境。",en:"This background is crucial: a considerable number of these dishes were lost first because even the “recipe” was not passed down. Milk-Brewed Fish survives only as a name in the Burning Tail Banquet menu, with no detailed method; for Chef Mao’s Three-Delicacy Soup, the “three delicacies” are impossible to determine because of missing records. This lack of documentation means that recreation work faces a “no evidence to rely on” dilemma from the very beginning."}]}]},{heading:{zh:"第二章 口味的革命：清雅传统与重口味的博弈",en:"Chapter Two: The Revolution of Taste — The Struggle Between Refined Tradition and Heavy Flavors"},paras:[{zh:"如果说文献散佚是“看不见的手”，那么口味变迁则是一股更加深远、更加不可逆转的力量。",en:"If the scattering of texts is an “invisible hand,” then the transformation of taste is an even deeper and more irreversible force."}],subs:[{subheading:{zh:"2.1 宋代文人的“清简本味”传统",en:"2.1 The Song Literati’s Tradition of “Clarity and Original Flavors”"},paras:[{zh:"宋代士大夫饮食的核心审美是“清简本味”。宋代文人在自然审美和乡居生活中表现出强烈的本味追求，“彼时偏好食物不调和的生活滋味”，推崇“清和为本”，极少重盐重酱。这种审美有其深刻的社会背景：宋代文化昌盛，读书人激增，仕途门槛抬高，许多文人壮志难酬，转而从日常生活的精致化中寻找精神寄托。饮食的清简化，正是文人阶层“淡泊明志”的精神投射。",en:"The core aesthetic of Song scholar-official cuisine was “clarity and original flavors.” Song literati, in their natural aesthetic and country life, showed a strong pursuit of original flavors — “they preferred the unseasoned taste of food,” advocating “harmony as the foundation,” with very little heavy salt or heavy soy sauce. This aesthetic had a profound social background: Song culture was flourishing, the number of educated people surged, the threshold for official careers rose, and many literati, unable to fulfill their ambitions, turned to the refinement of daily life for spiritual sustenance. The simplification of cuisine was precisely the spiritual projection of the literati class’s “tranquility and clear ambition.”"},{zh:"我的复刻菜中，宋代的东坡黄州煮鱼、蟹酿橙、东坡豆腐、玉糁羹、金饭、酥黄独、罗汉斋都深深植根于这一传统。以东坡黄州煮鱼为例：全程不加酱油、不加辣椒，仅靠姜、酒、橘皮去腥提鲜，追求“汤面只允许微微微动”的极致火候控制。这是典型的文人清雅风格——用最朴素的手段释放食材本身的鲜味。",en:"Among my recreated dishes, the Song dynasty dishes — Dongpo’s Huangzhou Boiled Fish, Crab Stuffed in Orange, Dongpo Tofu, Jade-Grain Soup, Golden Rice, Crispy Huangdu, and Buddha’s Delight — are all deeply rooted in this tradition. Take Dongpo’s Huangzhou Boiled Fish: no soy sauce, no chili, only ginger, wine, and orange peel to remove fishiness and enhance freshness, pursuing the extreme heat control of “the broth surface barely trembling.” This is a typical literati style — using the simplest means to release the ingredient’s own flavor."}]},{subheading:{zh:"2.2 美洲作物传入与口味的根本性转变",en:"2.2 The Introduction of American Crops and a Fundamental Shift in Taste"},paras:[{zh:"明代中后期，源自美洲的作物如玉米、番薯和辣椒等纷纷传入中国，并迅速扩散至长江流域，深刻影响了当地的饮食文化。辣椒原产于中南美洲，传入中国后，很长一段时间被视为观赏植物。但到了清中后期，辣椒逐渐沿长江向上中游扩散，“从江浙地区经过江西，一直延伸到湖广、四川、贵州等广袤地区，国人开始大范围食用辣椒”。",en:"In the mid-to-late Ming dynasty, crops from the Americas — corn, sweet potato, and chili pepper — were introduced to China and rapidly spread through the Yangtze River basin, profoundly influencing local food culture. Chili pepper, native to Central and South America, was for a long time regarded as an ornamental plant after its introduction to China. But by the mid-to-late Qing, chili gradually spread along the Yangtze River to the middle and upper reaches, “from Jiangsu and Zhejiang through Jiangxi, extending all the way to Huguang, Sichuan, Guizhou, and other vast regions; Chinese people began to consume chili on a large scale.”"},{zh:"辣椒的普及有着深刻的社会经济原因。在食盐匮乏的地区，“下饭”的食物需要靠酸味或者辣味来弥补，辣椒“不挑气候、土壤，在中国大多数地方收获期长达半年，口味又重，拿来下饭，再好不过”。然而，辣椒的流行也带来了一场味觉革命——人们的口味变得越来越重，越来越依赖辛香刺激。",en:"The popularity of chili had profound socioeconomic reasons. In regions where salt was scarce, “food that goes down well with rice” needed sour or spicy flavors to compensate; chili “doesn’t fuss about climate or soil, has a harvest period of up to half a year in most parts of China, and has a strong flavor — perfect for making food go down.” However, the popularity of chili also brought a taste revolution — people’s tastes became heavier and heavier, increasingly dependent on spicy stimulation."},{zh:"这种口味革命对清雅风格的古菜造成了致命打击。当辣椒、花椒、豆瓣酱成为餐桌上的主角时，东坡黄州煮鱼那种“不靠调料掩盖食材”的做法显得过于平淡。宋代没有辣椒，“一桌没有辣椒的菜肯定无法吸引今日四川的顾客”。这不仅是川菜的问题，而是整个饮食审美的转向——从“品味本真”转向“追求刺激”。",en:"This taste revolution dealt a fatal blow to dishes of the refined, elegant style. When chili, Sichuan peppercorn, and doubanjiang became the stars of the table, the approach of Dongpo’s Huangzhou Boiled Fish — “not masking ingredients with seasonings” — seemed too bland. The Song dynasty had no chili; “a table without chili definitely cannot attract today’s Sichuan customers.” This is not just a Sichuan issue; it is a shift in the entire culinary aesthetic — from “tasting the original” to “pursuing stimulation.”"}]},{subheading:{zh:"2.3 阶级分野与“上层口味”的消失",en:"2.3 Class Division and the Disappearance of “Upper-Class Taste”"},paras:[{zh:"一个耐人寻味的现象是，直到清朝灭亡前后，辣椒的流传人群仍“仅限于乡村老百姓”，“城里的饮食罕有辣味。至于贵族和世家，更不屑于尝试这种’低贱’的味道”，曾国藩甚至“偷偷”吃辣而愧对人言。",en:"A thought-provoking phenomenon is that until around the fall of the Qing dynasty, the spread of chili was still “limited to rural commoners”; “city food rarely had spicy flavors. As for nobles and great families, they disdained to try this 'lowly' taste.” Zeng Guofan even ate chili “secretly” and was ashamed to let others know."},{zh:"贵族世家的大厨们认为辣味“不符合中国传统的’食疗’原则，也不符合调和的品味原则，味觉元素过于突兀，且会破坏高级食材的原味”。然而，恰恰是这些“贵族世家”的精致菜肴——如蒋侍郎豆腐、随园豆豉黄鱼、红煨羊肉——在近现代的社会变革中最先消亡。辛亥革命后，世家大族瓦解，他们的私厨流散，那些“不以名贵食材炫技、而以精妙技艺和调和品味取胜”的菜品失去了赖以生存的社会土壤。",en:"The great chefs of noble families believed that spicy flavors “did not conform to traditional Chinese 'food therapy' principles, nor to the principle of harmonious taste; the taste elements were too abrupt and would destroy the original flavors of fine ingredients.” Yet it was precisely these “noble family” refined dishes — such as Minister Jiang’s Tofu, Suiyuan Yellow Croaker with Fermented Black Beans, and Red-Braised Lamb — that were the first to disappear in the modern social transformation. After the 1911 Revolution, the great families collapsed, their private chefs dispersed, and those dishes that “did not show off with rare ingredients but won through exquisite technique and harmonious taste” lost the social soil they depended on."},{zh:"而那些面向平民的重口味菜品——“江湖菜”——反而在近代商业化城市中迅速崛起。自中国近代开埠，城市工商业阶层崛起，“江湖菜”应运而生，“来自商人阶层，自有一种朴实热烈的气息”。各帮派的独门绝艺各有千秋——淮扬菜的刀工、四川菜的麻辣、粤菜的海鲜——这些“江湖菜”的浓烈风味逐渐成为主流，而清雅文人菜的生存空间被彻底挤压。",en:"Meanwhile, the heavy-flavored dishes aimed at common people — “jianghu cuisine” — rose rapidly in modern commercial cities. Since China’s modern port openings, an urban commercial class rose, and “jianghu cuisine” emerged, “coming from the merchant class, with a simple and passionate character.” Each school had its own unique skills — Huaiyang cuisine’s knife work, Sichuan cuisine’s spiciness, Cantonese cuisine’s seafood — and these “jianghu” strong flavors gradually became mainstream, while the living space of refined literati cuisine was completely squeezed out."}]}]},{heading:{zh:"第三章 食材之困：当原料本身消失或改变",en:"Chapter Three: The Predicament of Ingredients — When the Raw Materials Themselves Disappear or Change"},paras:[{zh:"即使文献完整、口味未变，食材本身的消失或变迁也会让古菜无法复刻。“食材之困”被公认为经典菜品还原失败的一大障碍。",en:"Even if the texts are complete and tastes unchanged, the disappearance or transformation of ingredients themselves can make ancient dishes impossible to recreate. The “ingredient predicament” is recognized as a major obstacle to restoring classic dishes."}],subs:[{subheading:{zh:"3.1 品种退化与生态环境改变",en:"3.1 Variety Degeneration and Ecological Change"},paras:[]},{subheading:{zh:"黄鱼的案例。",en:"The case of yellow croaker."},paras:[{zh:"随园豆豉黄鱼以新鲜大黄鱼为主角。大黄鱼曾是中国东海最重要的经济鱼类之一，清代江浙沿海产量丰富。但20世纪后期过度捕捞导致野生大黄鱼资源几近枯竭。食材的稀缺使得这道以黄鱼为主角的菜逐渐从家常餐桌消失——不是人们不想做，而是做不起了。",en:"Suiyuan Yellow Croaker with Fermented Black Beans features fresh large yellow croaker. The large yellow croaker was once one of the most important commercial fish in the East China Sea, abundant along the Jiangsu-Zhejiang coast in the Qing dynasty. But overfishing in the late 20th century nearly exhausted wild large yellow croaker resources. The scarcity of the ingredient caused this dish, which stars yellow croaker, to gradually disappear from home tables — not because people didn’t want to make it, but because they couldn’t afford to."}]},{subheading:{zh:"香榧子与黄独的案例。",en:"The case of torreya nuts and huangdu."},paras:[{zh:"东坡豆腐使用“研榧子”——研磨的香榧子；酥黄独的面糊也依赖香榧子。香榧树生长缓慢，果实产量低，价格昂贵，现代家庭烹饪几乎不会使用。而“黄独”在古代可能指山慈菇或某种特定芋类，现代已难以确定其确切品种。当核心食材变得稀缺或难以辨认时，依赖它们的菜品自然消亡。",en:"Dongpo Tofu uses “ground torreya nuts” — ground Torreya grandis nuts; the batter for Crispy Huangdu also relies on torreya nuts. The torreya tree grows slowly, its fruit yield is low, and its price is high; modern home cooking almost never uses it. And “huangdu” in ancient times may have referred to mountain arrowhead or some specific taro, but today its exact variety is difficult to determine. When core ingredients become scarce or unrecognizable, the dishes that depend on them naturally disappear."}]},{subheading:{zh:"3.2 食材保护与替代的两难",en:"3.2 The Dilemma of Ingredient Protection and Substitution"},paras:[{zh:"当代复刻工作中，“食材之困”表现得尤为突出。广州“消失的名菜”项目在复刻民国粤菜时，遇到了典型困境：“玉簪插罗裙”需要的主要食材田鸡，从1989年开始被列为国家二级保护动物，不能食用。大厨们尝试用牛蛙代替，但“牛蛙体形大、脂肪层厚、肉质松散且纤维粗”，还原效果“无论是卖相还是风味，都无法达到预期效果”。",en:"In contemporary recreation work, the “ingredient predicament” is particularly prominent. The Guangzhou “Lost Famous Dishes” project, in recreating Republican-era Cantonese cuisine, encountered a typical dilemma: “Jade Hairpin Inserted in Silk Skirt” requires frog as its main ingredient, but since 1989 the frog has been listed as a Class II protected species in China and cannot be eaten. The chefs tried substituting bullfrog, but “bullfrog is large, has a thick fat layer, loose meat, and coarse fibers,” and the restoration effect “whether in appearance or flavor, could not achieve the expected result.”"},{zh:"类似的问题也存在于我复刻的古菜中。古代使用的河鲜品种、蔬菜品种、家禽家畜品种，与今天市场上能买到的已经发生了巨大变化。宋代山芋与今天的芋头可能存在品种差异；清代黄芽菜“以北方来者为佳”，但现代蔬菜品种改良后，原版风味难以复现。",en:"Similar problems exist among the dishes I recreated. The river fish, vegetables, poultry, and livestock varieties used in ancient times have undergone enormous changes compared to what is available in today’s markets. Song dynasty taro may differ from today’s taro; Qing dynasty yellow sprout cabbage “was considered best from the north,” but after modern vegetable breeding, the original flavor is difficult to reproduce."}]}]},{heading:{zh:"第四章 技艺的门槛：当“手艺”无法被标准化",en:"Chapter Four: The Threshold of Technique — When “Craft” Cannot Be Standardized"},paras:[{zh:"有些古菜并未失传于文献缺失或食材消失，而是因为它们的烹饪技艺要求太高，无法通过标准化方式传承。",en:"Some ancient dishes were not lost due to missing texts or disappearing ingredients, but because their cooking techniques were too demanding to be passed down through standardization."}],subs:[{subheading:{zh:"4.1 火候的极致要求",en:"4.1 Extreme Demands on Heat Control"},paras:[{zh:"油爆羊肚要求“滚水略炸，入热油锅，连翻数次”，全程仅数十秒。这种依赖厨师瞬时判断的技法，“在现代餐饮标准化体系中难以复刻”。东坡黄州煮鱼的“冷水下锅”“全程不搅动”“汤面只允许微微微动”同样是极其苛刻的火候要求，依赖个人经验而非精确量化，很难写成标准化菜谱。",en:"Quick-Fried Lamb Tripe requires “blanch in boiling water briefly, then into a hot oil wok, tossing several times,” the whole process lasting only tens of seconds. This technique, which depends on the chef’s instantaneous judgment, “is difficult to replicate in modern standardized catering systems.” Dongpo’s Huangzhou Boiled Fish requires “cold water,” “no stirring throughout,” and “the broth surface barely trembling” — equally demanding heat control, dependent on personal experience rather than precise quantification, and very difficult to write into a standardized recipe."},{zh:"在还原试验中，复刻团队发现古法技艺“需要多年的实践经验才能掌握得恰到好处”。像“绍酒逼牛腩”需要用湿泥包裹食材进行烹饪，“对火候、时间以及泥的材质和厚度都有极高的要求”，火候稍过或稍欠都会导致失败。",en:"In restoration experiments, recreation teams found that ancient techniques “require many years of practical experience to master just right.” For example, “beef brisket pressed in Shaoxing wine” requires wrapping the ingredient in wet mud for cooking, “with extremely high demands on heat, time, and the material and thickness of the mud”; a little too much or too little heat will cause failure."}]},{subheading:{zh:"4.2 复杂工序与时间成本",en:"4.2 Complex Procedures and Time Cost"},paras:[{zh:"蒋侍郎豆腐要求豆腐去皮、切十六片、晾干、猪油煎至“清烟起”、虾米精确到“一百二十个”、葱段“一百二十段”、分次加甜酒、秋油、糖“再滚一回”......这种近乎苛刻的精细度，使得普通家庭难以复刻。",en:"Minister Jiang’s Tofu requires peeling the tofu, cutting it into sixteen slices, air-drying, frying in lard until “clear smoke rises,” exactly “one hundred and twenty” dried shrimp, “one hundred and twenty” scallion segments, and adding sweet wine, soy sauce, and sugar in stages, “bringing to a boil again” each time... This almost obsessive precision makes it difficult for ordinary households to recreate."},{zh:"随园黄芽菜煨火腿更要求“连煨半日”。在现代快节奏生活中，这种时间成本几乎不可能被接受。清代袁枚本人也强调黄芽菜“一熟便吃，迟则色味俱变”，对食用时机的严格要求进一步增加了传承难度。",en:"Suiyuan Yellow Sprout Cabbage Simmered with Ham even requires “simmering for half a day.” In the fast pace of modern life, this time cost is almost impossible to accept. Qing dynasty Yuan Mei himself emphasized that yellow sprout cabbage should be “eaten as soon as it is cooked; if delayed, both color and flavor change,” and the strict demand on timing further increased the difficulty of transmission."}]},{subheading:{zh:"4.3 从“慢煮”到“爆炒”的技法转向",en:"4.3 The Shift from “Slow Simmering” to “Quick-Frying”"},paras:[{zh:"中国古代烹饪技法的演变本身就是一个“淘汰”过程。先秦至汉以羹汤为主，讲究“水火之济”；唐宋以后，随着冶金技术、榨油技术、燃料和城市商业的发展，“炒”法逐渐普及。爆、炒、熘、炸等技法“需要猛火急攻，瞬间锁住食材水分与鲜味”。",en:"The evolution of ancient Chinese cooking techniques is itself a process of “elimination.” From the pre-Qin to the Han, soups and stews dominated, emphasizing “the harmony of water and fire.” After the Tang and Song, with the development of metallurgy, oil pressing, fuel, and urban commerce, “stir-frying” gradually became widespread. Techniques such as bao (explosive stir-frying), chao (stir-frying), liu (quick-frying with sauce), and zha (deep-frying) “require fierce fire and rapid attack, instantly locking in the ingredient’s moisture and freshness.”"},{zh:"这一技法转向对明代爆菜体系造成了直接影响。明代是中国“爆”法烹饪的高峰期，《宋氏养生部》中记录了油爆猪、油爆鹅、油爆鸡等多种爆菜。但随着后世烹饪重心向炖、蒸、红烧等技法转移，明代那种精细的“油爆”体系已经失传。油爆羊肚的消亡，不仅是单一菜品的消亡，而是整个烹饪技法体系的断裂。",en:"This technical shift directly affected the Ming dynasty “bao” cuisine system. The Ming was the peak of Chinese “bao” cooking; Song Family Dietary Methods records oil-burst pork, oil-burst goose, oil-burst chicken, and many other bao dishes. But as later cooking focused more on stewing, steaming, and red-braising, the refined “oil-burst” system of the Ming was lost. The disappearance of Quick-Fried Lamb Tripe is not just the disappearance of a single dish, but the rupture of an entire cooking technique system."}]}]},{heading:{zh:"第五章 传承的断裂：私人化知识与社会动荡",en:"Chapter Five: The Rupture of Transmission — Private Knowledge and Social Upheaval"},paras:[],subs:[{subheading:{zh:"5.1 私人化的知识传承",en:"5.1 Private Knowledge Transmission"},paras:[{zh:"许多古菜的传承依赖于特定的个人或家族，一旦传承链条断裂，菜品便面临永久失传的风险。",en:"Many ancient dishes depended on specific individuals or families for their transmission; once the chain broke, the dish faced permanent loss."},{zh:"北魏崔浩的《食经》就是典型案例。这部书的内容并非崔浩个人创作，而是其母亲卢氏及家族女性长辈口述的烹饪技艺总结。崔浩在《食经叙》中写道：“先妣虑久废忘，后生无知见，而少不习业书，乃占授为九篇”。这种“家族女性口传、男性笔录”的传承模式，一旦家族遭遇变故或后代对烹饪失去兴趣，知识便难以延续。",en:"Cui Hao’s Shijing from the Northern Wei is a typical case. The content of the book was not Cui Hao’s own creation, but an oral summary of cooking techniques by his mother, Lady Lu, and other female elders of the family. Cui Hao wrote in the Shijing Xu: “My late mother feared that these would be forgotten over time, and that later generations would have no knowledge of them; since I was young and had not studied professionally, she dictated them to me in nine sections.” This transmission model — “oral from family women, recorded by men” — meant that once the family suffered misfortune or descendants lost interest in cooking, the knowledge could hardly continue."},{zh:"臞仙烧茄子的情况类似。朱权以“臞仙”为号，其饮食著作主要为宁王府内部使用。随着宁王府的衰落和明代藩王制度的瓦解，这些菜谱逐渐散佚。",en:"The case of Quxian’s Braised Eggplant is similar. Zhu Quan used “Quxian” as his sobriquet, and his dietary writings were mainly for internal use in the Prince of Ning’s household. With the decline of the Prince of Ning’s household and the collapse of the Ming princely system, these recipes gradually scattered."},{zh:"毛厨三鲜汤以毛姓厨师命名，其传承高度依赖该厨师的个人技艺和知名度。这类“个人品牌菜”的传承链条非常脆弱——“一旦厨师本人离世或其后人未继承技艺，菜品便面临失传风险”。",en:"Chef Mao’s Three-Delicacy Soup is named after a chef surnamed Mao, and its transmission depended heavily on that chef’s personal skill and reputation. Such “personal brand dishes” have very fragile transmission chains — “once the chef himself dies or his descendants do not inherit the skill, the dish faces the risk of being lost.”"}]},{subheading:{zh:"5.2 社会动荡的直接冲击",en:"5.2 The Direct Impact of Social Upheaval"},paras:[{zh:"战争和饥荒不仅是文献散佚的原因，也直接造成了饮食文化的断层。北魏崔浩撰写《食经》的直接动机，正是因为“遭逢乱世与饥荒”，家族“丧乱饥馑已久，日常饮食以蔬食糊口，导致四时祭祀等礼法活动难以维持”。",en:"War and famine were not only causes of textual loss but also directly caused ruptures in food culture. Cui Hao’s direct motivation for writing the Shijing was precisely because “he encountered a time of chaos and famine,” and the family “had long suffered from disorder and hunger, eating only simple vegetable food in daily life, so that seasonal ritual activities could not be maintained.”"},{zh:"研究显示，3至6世纪关中地区的社会动荡导致了人类饮食结构的显著变化，动物蛋白的获取变得“减少且不稳定”，反映出“战争和社会动荡对畜牧业和生活水平的破坏性影响”。当社会连基本的饮食保障都无法维持时，精致的烹饪技艺自然无从传承。",en:"Research shows that social turmoil in the Guanzhong region from the 3rd to 6th centuries led to significant changes in human diet, with animal protein acquisition becoming “reduced and unstable,” reflecting “the destructive impact of war and social turmoil on animal husbandry and living standards.” When society cannot even maintain basic food security, refined cooking techniques naturally cannot be transmitted."},{zh:"近代的案例同样触目惊心。1937年抗日战争爆发，一些餐馆被迫关闭，导致某些烹饪技艺彻底失传，直到70多年后才被民间人士通过实验复原。社会动荡对饮食文化传承的冲击是毁灭性的——它不仅中断了当下的烹饪实践，更切断了代际之间的知识传递。",en:"Modern cases are equally shocking. In 1937, when the War of Resistance Against Japan (the Second Sino-Japanese War) broke out, some restaurants were forced to close, causing certain cooking techniques to be completely lost, only to be restored by private individuals through experimentation more than seventy years later. The impact of social upheaval on culinary transmission is devastating — it not only interrupts current cooking practice but also cuts off the intergenerational transfer of knowledge."}]}]},{heading:{zh:"第六章 文化语境的消失：当“意义”不再",en:"Chapter Six: The Disappearance of Cultural Context — When “Meaning” Is No More"},paras:[{zh:"有些古菜的消亡，不是因为食材没了、技艺丢了，而是因为赋予它们意义的文化语境消失了。",en:"Some ancient dishes disappeared not because ingredients were gone or techniques lost, but because the cultural context that gave them meaning had vanished."}],subs:[{subheading:{zh:"6.1 文人雅集的消亡",en:"6.1 The Disappearance of Literati Gatherings"},paras:[{zh:"酥黄独的诞生情境是“雪夜芋正熟，有仇子载酒来”——一种冬日雪夜、友人携酒来访、就地取材的文人雅集。这种饮食情境在现代社会中几乎不可能复制。菜还在，但那种“雪夜围炉、以芋待客”的生活方式已经不复存在。",en:"Crispy Huangdu was born in the setting of “a snowy night when the taro was just cooked, and a friend named Qiu arrived with wine” — a winter snowy night, a friend bringing wine, using local ingredients for a literati gathering. This kind of dining situation is almost impossible to replicate in modern society. The dish remains, but the lifestyle of “gathering around the stove on a snowy night, serving taro to guests” no longer exists."}]},{subheading:{zh:"6.2 花卉入馔传统的断裂",en:"6.2 The Rupture of the Tradition of Eating Flowers"},paras:[{zh:"金饭以菊花入馔。宋代文人将花卉入馔视为雅事，菊花在宋代文化中与梅、兰、竹并称“四君子”。但明清以后，花卉入馔逐渐从日常饮食中退出，仅保留在少数节令食品中。金饭的消亡，本质上是花卉入馔这一文化传统的消亡。",en:"Golden Rice uses chrysanthemums. Song literati regarded eating flowers as an elegant pursuit; in Song culture, chrysanthemum was one of the “Four Gentlemen” along with plum, orchid, and bamboo. But after the Ming and Qing, eating flowers gradually withdrew from daily diet, remaining only in a few seasonal foods. The disappearance of Golden Rice is essentially the disappearance of the tradition of eating flowers."}]},{subheading:{zh:"6.3 宗教饮食体系的边缘化",en:"6.3 The Marginalization of the Religious Dietary System"},paras:[{zh:"罗汉斋是寺院素菜的代表，取名自十八罗汉聚集一堂。但随着佛教寺院从“自给自足”的饮食模式转向商业化运营，以及素食在普通饮食中占比下降，“罗汉斋”的宗教文化内涵逐渐淡化，菜品本身的文化吸引力也随之减弱。",en:"Buddha’s Delight is a representative temple vegetarian dish, named after the eighteen arhats gathering together. But as Buddhist temples shifted from a “self-sufficient” dietary model to commercial operation, and as the proportion of vegetarian food in ordinary diets declined, the religious and cultural connotation of “Buddha’s Delight” gradually faded, and the dish’s cultural appeal diminished accordingly."}]},{subheading:{zh:"6.4 兄弟对饮与朴素中的至味",en:"6.4 Brotherly Drinking and the Supreme Taste in Simplicity"},paras:[{zh:"玉糁羹的诞生背景，不是贬谪，而是一场兄弟之间的酒酣之乐。",en:"The background of Jade-Grain Soup is not exile, but a moment of brotherly drinking."},{zh:"《山家清供》记：“东坡一夕与子由饮，酣甚，槌芦菔烂煮，不用他料，只研白米为糁。”苏轼与弟弟苏辙感情极深，二人一生聚少离多，乌台诗案后更是各自贬谪。能在某个夜晚对坐饮酒、喝到酣畅，对他们而言本身就是难得的慰藉。就在这样的情境下，苏轼随手把萝卜捣烂、把白米研碎，煮了一锅什么都不加的羹。",en:"Shanjia Qinggong records: “One evening, Dongpo drank with Ziyou. When they were quite drunk, he pounded radish and boiled it thoroughly, using no other ingredients, only grinding white rice into coarse grains.” Su Shi and his younger brother Su Zhe (Ziyou) were extremely close. They spent most of their lives apart, and after the Wutai Poetry Case they were each exiled. To sit and drink together on a certain night, drinking until tipsy, was itself a rare comfort for them. In such a situation, Su Shi casually mashed radish, ground rice, and cooked a soup with nothing else added."},{zh:"吃完之后，他放下筷子，抚着案几叹道：“若非天竺酥酡，人间决无此味。”",en:"After eating, he put down his chopsticks, touched the table, and sighed: “If not for the heavenly suduo, there is no such flavor in the human world.”"},{zh:"这句话值得细品。“天竺酥酡”是佛教传说中天界的酥酪，人间不可能有。苏轼的意思不是“这碗羹像天界美食”，而是反过来——除了天界的酥酡，人间再没有比这更好的味道了。一碗萝卜碎米羹，被他推到了人间至味的位置。",en:"This sentence is worth savoring. “Heavenly suduo” is a kind of ambrosia from Buddhist legend, impossible in the human world. Su Shi did not mean “this soup is like heavenly food,” but rather the opposite — apart from heavenly suduo, there is no better taste in the human world. A bowl of radish and crushed rice soup was elevated by him to the position of supreme human flavor."},{zh:"这种“至味”从何而来？不是来自食材的珍贵，也不是来自调味的复杂。萝卜是最便宜的蔬菜，碎米是最普通的主食，做法只是“烂煮”，连盐都不放。它的美味来自三个层面：食材本身的本味、酒酣之后的感官状态、兄弟对坐的情感加成。",en:"Where does this “supreme taste” come from? Not from precious ingredients, nor from complex seasoning. Radish is the cheapest vegetable, crushed rice the most ordinary staple; the method is simply “boiling thoroughly,” without even salt. Its deliciousness comes from three levels: the original flavor of the ingredients themselves, the sensory state after drinking, and the emotional addition of brothers sitting together."},{zh:"这道菜的文化意义，恰恰在于它的“日常性”。它不是宴席菜，不是文人雅集的表演，而是一个人在最放松的时刻，用最朴素的方式，和最重要的人分享的一碗羹。这种“在日常中体味至味”的生活态度，是宋代文人饮食观的核心，也是苏轼一生颠沛中始终未失的精神底色。",en:"The cultural significance of this dish lies precisely in its “ordinariness.” It is not a banquet dish, not a performance at a literati gathering, but a bowl of soup shared by one person with the most important person in his life, in his most relaxed moment, using the simplest method. This attitude of “tasting the supreme in the everyday” is the core of Song literati food philosophy and the spiritual foundation that Su Shi never lost throughout his life of wandering."},{zh:"后人复刻这道菜时，即使做出相同的味道，也难以完全体会苏轼当时的心境。但“难以完全体会”不等于“无法理解”——只要我们还记得苏轼与苏辙的故事，还记得那个酒酣之夜，这碗萝卜碎米羹就不仅仅是一道菜，而是一个可以被反复讲述的文化记忆。",en:"When later generations recreate this dish, even if they make the same flavor, they cannot fully experience Su Shi’s state of mind at the time. But “difficult to fully experience” does not mean “impossible to understand” — as long as we remember the story of Su Shi and Su Zhe, and remember that drunken night, this bowl of radish and crushed rice soup is not just a dish, but a cultural memory that can be retold."},{zh:"这也正是“饮食寻根”的意义所在：我们寻找的不只是味道，更是味道背后的人、情感和生活方式。",en:"This is precisely the meaning of “culinary root-searching”: what we seek is not just flavor, but the people, emotions, and ways of life behind the flavor."}]}]},{heading:{zh:"第七章 综合分析与传承思考",en:"Chapter Seven: Comprehensive Analysis and Thoughts on Transmission"},withTable:!0,paras:[],subs:[{subheading:{zh:"7.1 失传原因分类",en:"7.1 Classification of the Causes of Loss"},paras:[{zh:"基于以上分析，我将这些菜的失传原因归纳为以下六类：",en:"Based on the above analysis, I classify the reasons for the loss of these dishes into six categories:"}]},{subheading:{zh:"一、文献散佚型（原方失传）：",en:"One, textual loss (original recipe lost):"},paras:[{zh:"乳酿鱼（烧尾宴食单仅有菜名）、毛厨三鲜汤（“三鲜”所指不明）、随园豆豉黄鱼（“同滚”表述模糊）、宋府烧蟹（具体配方不明）。",en:"Milk-Brewed Fish (only a name in the Burning Tail Banquet menu), Chef Mao’s Three-Delicacy Soup (the “three delicacies” unidentified), Suiyuan Yellow Croaker with Fermented Black Beans (“bring to a boil together” vague), Song Family Braised Crab (specific recipe unknown)."}]},{subheading:{zh:"二、口味变迁型（清雅被重口取代）：",en:"Two, taste change (refined replaced by heavy flavors):"},paras:[{zh:"东坡黄州煮鱼、蟹酿橙、东坡豆腐、金饭、玉糁羹、蒋侍郎豆腐、随园黄芽菜。这些菜的核心审美是“清简本味”，与后世重油重酱重辣的饮食趋势相悖。",en:"Dongpo’s Huangzhou Boiled Fish, Crab Stuffed in Orange, Dongpo Tofu, Golden Rice, Jade-Grain Soup, Minister Jiang’s Tofu, Suiyuan Yellow Sprout Cabbage. The core aesthetic of these dishes is “clarity and original flavors,” contrary to the later trend of heavy oil, heavy sauce, and heavy spiciness."}]},{subheading:{zh:"三、食材稀缺型（原料消失或变迁）：",en:"Three, ingredient scarcity (raw materials disappeared or changed):"},paras:[{zh:"随园豆豉黄鱼（野生大黄鱼枯竭）、酥黄独（黄独品种不明）、东坡豆腐（香榧子稀缺）、乳酿鱼（乳酪入菜传统消亡）。",en:"Suiyuan Yellow Croaker with Fermented Black Beans (wild large yellow croaker exhausted), Crispy Huangdu (huangdu variety unknown), Dongpo Tofu (torreya nuts scarce), Milk-Brewed Fish (dairy-in-cooking tradition vanished)."}]},{subheading:{zh:"四、技艺门槛型（工序复杂、火候苛刻）：",en:"Four, technique threshold (complex procedures, demanding heat control):"},paras:[{zh:"油爆羊肚、蓑衣饼、裙带面、蒋侍郎豆腐（精确到“一百二十段葱”）。",en:"Quick-Fried Lamb Tripe, Straw-Raincoat Cake, Ribbon Noodles, Minister Jiang’s Tofu (precise to “one hundred and twenty scallion segments”)."}]},{subheading:{zh:"五、传承断裂型（依赖特定个人或家族）：",en:"Five, transmission rupture (dependent on specific individuals or families):"},paras:[{zh:"臞仙烧茄子（藩王府衰落）、毛厨三鲜汤（个人品牌菜）、冻豆腐煨肉汤（元代宫廷菜谱未入民间）、糟蟹（酒糟制作技艺萎缩）。",en:"Quxian’s Braised Eggplant (princely household decline), Chef Mao’s Three-Delicacy Soup (personal brand dish), Frozen Tofu Simmered with Pork (Yuan court recipe never entered folk transmission), Wine-Lees Crab (wine-lees production technique shrinking)."}]},{subheading:{zh:"六、文化语境消失型（意义结构断裂）：",en:"Six, cultural context disappearance (meaning structure ruptured):"},paras:[{zh:"金饭（花卉入馔传统断裂）、酥黄独（雪夜雅集情境消失）、罗汉斋（寺院饮食文化边缘化）、玉糁羹（兄弟对饮情境不可复制）。",en:"Golden Rice (flower-eating tradition ruptured), Crispy Huangdu (snowy-night gathering context vanished), Buddha’s Delight (temple food culture marginalized), Jade-Grain Soup (brotherly drinking context unrepeatable)."},{zh:"需要说明的是，许多菜品的失传是多重原因叠加的结果。例如蒋侍郎豆腐既受口味变迁影响，也面临技艺门槛和传承断裂的问题；蟹酿橙既因口味变迁而边缘化，也受“苦酒”配方失传的制约。",en:"It should be noted that many dishes were lost due to a combination of multiple causes. For example, Minister Jiang’s Tofu was affected by both taste change and technique threshold and transmission rupture; Crab Stuffed in Orange was marginalized by taste change and also constrained by the loss of the “bitter wine” recipe."}]},{subheading:{zh:"7.2 从历史分期看失传的节奏",en:"7.2 The Rhythm of Loss by Historical Period"},paras:[{zh:"将这些菜按朝代排列，可以观察到一个有趣的规律：",en:"Arranging these dishes by dynasty reveals an interesting pattern:"}]},{subheading:{zh:"唐宋时期：",en:"The Tang–Song period:"},paras:[{zh:"这个时期的菜品失传主要源于“口味革命”和“文献散佚”。宋代菜品大量依赖清雅本味的审美，而这种审美在明清以后被重口味趋势所取代。同时，宋代以前烹饪文献的大量散佚，使得许多菜品的具体做法无从考证。",en:"The loss of dishes in this period mainly stems from the “taste revolution” and “textual loss.” Song dishes largely depended on the aesthetic of clarity and original flavors, which was replaced by the heavy-flavor trend after the Ming and Qing. At the same time, the massive loss of pre-Song culinary texts made the specific methods of many dishes unverifiable."}]},{subheading:{zh:"元代：",en:"The Yuan dynasty:"},paras:[{zh:"元代是蒙古族统治时期，其饮食文化带有鲜明的游牧特色。《饮膳正要》记录的菜品多为“宫廷专属”，未能进入民间传承体系。元代菜品的失传，更多是“文化断层”而非“口味淘汰”——元朝灭亡后，其宫廷饮食文化随之消亡。",en:"The Yuan was a period of Mongol rule, and its food culture had distinct nomadic characteristics. The dishes recorded in Yinshan Zhengyao were mostly “court-exclusive” and did not enter the folk transmission system. The loss of Yuan dishes is more a “cultural rupture” than “taste elimination” — after the fall of the Yuan, its court food culture disappeared with it."}]},{subheading:{zh:"明代：",en:"The Ming dynasty:"},paras:[{zh:"明代菜品面临的主要挑战是“技艺体系断裂”和“传承链条脆弱”。臞仙烧茄子因藩王府衰落而失传，油爆羊肚因爆菜体系整体衰落而消亡，宋府烧蟹则受蟹馔烹饪体系整体衰落的影响。",en:"Ming dishes faced the main challenges of “technique system rupture” and “fragile transmission chains.” Quxian’s Braised Eggplant was lost due to the decline of the princely household, Quick-Fried Lamb Tripe disappeared due to the overall decline of the bao cuisine system, and Song Family Braised Crab was affected by the overall decline of the crab cuisine system."}]},{subheading:{zh:"清代：",en:"The Qing dynasty:"},paras:[{zh:"清代菜品的失传呈现出最复杂的面向。一方面，清代是古代烹饪技艺的集大成时期，《随园食单》等著作记录了丰富的菜品；另一方面，恰恰是这些精致的菜品，在近现代的社会变革中最先消亡。世家大族的瓦解、“江湖菜”的崛起、快节奏生活的冲击，共同构成了清代精致菜品消亡的合力。",en:"The loss of Qing dishes presents the most complex picture. On the one hand, the Qing was the period of greatest integration of ancient cooking techniques, and works such as Suiyuan Shidan recorded a wealth of dishes. On the other hand, it was precisely these refined dishes that were the first to disappear in the modern social transformation. The collapse of great families, the rise of “jianghu cuisine,” and the impact of fast-paced life together formed the combined force that caused the disappearance of refined Qing dishes."}]},{subheading:{zh:"7.3 传承的可能路径",en:"7.3 Possible Paths for Transmission"},paras:[{zh:"那么，这些古菜是否完全无法重现？并非如此。当代已有不少成功的复刻案例。",en:"So, are these ancient dishes completely impossible to revive? Not at all. There have already been many successful recreation cases in contemporary times."},{zh:"研究者王仁兴积三十年之功，“从传世典籍、出土文献和考古出土的古炊器及墓室壁画中，梳理出自商代至清代一千多道代表性名菜的相关记录及做法”。生活史作家徐鲤、卢冉通过《宋宴》《元宴》，“亲手复制、烹饪了百余道现已失传的史书中的菜品”。广州博物馆与中国大酒店合作的“消失的名菜”项目，通过“在尊重传统的基础上，巧妙融合现代技艺，结合不同食材的特点，将古籍中的菜式进行创新性融合，创造性重塑”。",en:"Researcher Wang Renxing spent thirty years “sorting out the records and methods of more than a thousand representative famous dishes from the Shang dynasty to the Qing from transmitted texts, excavated documents, ancient cooking vessels unearthed by archaeology, and tomb murals.” Food historians Xu Li and Lu Ran, through Song Banquet and Yuan Banquet, “personally copied and cooked more than a hundred dishes from historical texts that had been lost.” The Guangzhou Museum and China Hotel’s “Lost Famous Dishes” project has brought a series of vanished dishes back to the table by “respecting tradition while cleverly integrating modern techniques, combining the characteristics of different ingredients, and innovatively blending and creatively reshaping ancient recipes.”"},{zh:"这些复刻实践提示了几条可行的传承路径：",en:"These recreation practices suggest several feasible paths for transmission:"}]},{subheading:{zh:"第一，文献考据与现代烹饪科学的结合。",en:"First, combining textual research with modern culinary science."},paras:[{zh:"对于文献记载不完整的菜品，可以通过食材分析和烹饪原理推断原方。蒋侍郎豆腐已被南京图书馆馆员成功复原，证明其并非完全无法重现。",en:"For dishes with incomplete records, the original recipe can be inferred through ingredient analysis and cooking principles. Minister Jiang’s Tofu has been successfully restored by a Nanjing Library librarian, proving that it is not completely impossible to revive."}]},{subheading:{zh:"第二，合理替代而非机械还原。",en:"Second, reasonable substitution rather than mechanical restoration."},paras:[{zh:"对于食材稀缺或受保护的菜品，可以用风味相近的食材替代（如用牛蛙替代田鸡），在尊重历史的基础上做出适应性调整。",en:"For dishes with scarce or protected ingredients, ingredients with similar flavors can be substituted (such as using bullfrog instead of frog), making adaptive adjustments while respecting history."}]},{subheading:{zh:"第三，文化叙事的重建。",en:"Third, rebuilding cultural narrative."},paras:[{zh:"对于文化语境消失的菜品，可以通过讲述菜品背后的历史故事、文人雅集和情感意义，重建其文化价值。这正是我的网站“古味寻根”所尝试的方向——用中英双语向中外访客讲述每一道菜的文化故事。",en:"For dishes whose cultural context has disappeared, their cultural value can be rebuilt by telling the historical stories, literati gatherings, and emotional meanings behind the dishes. This is precisely the direction my website “Tracing Culinary Roots” attempts — using bilingual Chinese-English content to tell the cultural stories of each dish to visitors from China and abroad."}]},{subheading:{zh:"第四，数字化传播。",en:"Fourth, digital dissemination."},paras:[{zh:"当代数字技术和社交媒体为古菜的传播提供了前所未有的可能。一个中英双语的网站，可以让世界各地的访客了解中国古代失传菜的魅力——这在苏轼的时代是不可想象的。",en:"Contemporary digital technology and social media provide unprecedented possibilities for the spread of ancient dishes. A bilingual Chinese-English website can let visitors from around the world learn about the charm of ancient Chinese lost dishes — something unimaginable in Su Shi’s time."}]}]},{heading:{zh:"结语",en:"Conclusion"},paras:[{zh:"这些古菜的消亡，是中国饮食文化变迁的一个缩影。它们有的死于文献的散佚，有的死于口味的革命，有的死于食材的消失，有的死于技艺的断裂，有的死于文化的变迁。每一个“死亡”背后，都折射出中国历史发展的某一侧面——战乱与太平、开放与保守、精致与实用、传统与变革的角力。",en:"The disappearance of these ancient dishes is a microcosm of the transformation of Chinese food culture. Some died from the scattering of texts, some from the revolution of taste, some from the disappearance of ingredients, some from the rupture of technique, some from cultural change. Behind each “death” is reflected a certain aspect of China’s historical development — the struggle between war and peace, openness and conservatism, refinement and practicality, tradition and transformation."},{zh:"然而，“失传”不等于“永久消失”。每一次有意识的复刻，都是对历史的一次打捞，对文化的一次抢救。当我们重新做出一道宋代文人煮鱼、一道清代官员豆腐时，我们不仅在还原一种味道，更在连接一条跨越千年的文化脉络。这条脉络的一端是苏轼在黄州清贫中的从容，另一端是我——一个高中生——在厨房里的探索。古今之间，味道相连。",en:"However, “lost” does not mean “permanently gone.” Every conscious recreation is a salvage of history, a rescue of culture. When we recreate a Song literati’s boiled fish or a Qing official’s tofu, we are not only restoring a flavor but also connecting a cultural thread spanning a thousand years. One end of this thread is Su Shi’s calm in poverty in Huangzhou; the other end is me — a high school student — exploring in the kitchen. Between ancient and modern, flavors connect."}]},{heading:{zh:"参考文献",en:"References"},paras:[],list:[{zh:"宋·苏轼《苏轼文集》卷七十三《煮鱼法》",en:"Su Shi, Collected Works of Su Shi, Vol. 73, “Method of Boiling Fish”"},{zh:"宋·林洪《山家清供》",en:"Lin Hong, Shanjia Qinggong"},{zh:"宋·朱彧《萍洲可谈》卷二",en:"Zhu Yu, Pingzhou Ketan, Vol. 2"},{zh:"北魏·崔浩《食经》（散佚，见《齐民要术》引文）",en:"Cui Hao (Northern Wei), Shijing (lost; see quotations in Qimin Yaoshu)"},{zh:"元·忽思慧《饮膳正要》",en:"Hu Sihui (Yuan), Yinshan Zhengyao"},{zh:"明·宋诩《宋氏养生部》",en:"Song Xu (Ming), Song Family Dietary Methods"},{zh:"明·朱权《臞仙神隐书》",en:"Zhu Quan (Ming), Quxian Shenyin (Book of the Emaciated Immortal)"},{zh:"清·袁枚《随园食单》",en:"Yuan Mei (Qing), Suiyuan Shidan"},{zh:"陈姝《中国古代饮食的本味追求》，南开大学历史学院",en:"Chen Shu, “The Pursuit of Original Flavor in Ancient Chinese Diet,” School of History, Nankai University"},{zh:"姚伟钧《明清辣食尚：异域食材与长江饮食文化的融合创新》，《光明日报》2024年5月10日",en:"Yao Weijun, “The Ming-Qing Spicy Food Trend: The Fusion and Innovation of Foreign Ingredients and Yangtze River Food Culture,” Guangming Daily, May 10, 2024"},{zh:"郑小娟《辣椒是如何风靡全国的》，《文摘报》2019年8月24日",en:"Zheng Xiaojuan, “How Chili Peppers Swept the Nation,” Wenzhai Bao, August 24, 2019"},{zh:"广州博物馆与中国大酒店“消失的名菜”项目系列报道",en:"Guangzhou Museum and China Hotel, “Lost Famous Dishes” project series reports"},{zh:"生活史作家徐鲤、卢冉《宋宴》《元宴》",en:"Food historians Xu Li and Lu Ran, Song Banquet and Yuan Banquet"},{zh:"研究者王仁兴古代名菜复原研究",en:"Researcher Wang Renxing, research on the restoration of ancient famous dishes"}]}],i=[{key:"texts",zh:"文献散佚",en:"Loss of Texts",descZh:"原方失传，只剩菜名或记载不明",descEn:"Original recipes lost; only names or unclear records remain",examples:["ru-niang-yu","chef-mao-three-delicacy-soup","suiyuan-black-bean-yellow-croaker","song-family-braised-crab"]},{key:"taste",zh:"口味变迁",en:"Changing Tastes",descZh:"清雅本味被重油重酱重辣取代",descEn:"The refined aesthetic displaced by heavy oil, sauce and spice",examples:["dongpo-huangzhou-boiled-fish","crab-stuffed-orange","dongpo-tofu","golden-rice","jade-grain-soup","jiang-shilang-tofu","suiyuan-yellow-cabbage"]},{key:"ingredient",zh:"食材稀缺",en:"Scarce Ingredients",descZh:"原料消失、枯竭或品种变迁",descEn:"Ingredients vanished, exhausted, or changed in variety",examples:["suiyuan-black-bean-yellow-croaker","crispy-huangdu","dongpo-tofu","ru-niang-yu"]},{key:"technique",zh:"技艺门槛",en:"Technique Threshold",descZh:"工序复杂、火候苛刻，难以标准化",descEn:"Complex procedures and exacting heat control resist standardization",examples:["oil-burst-lamb-tripe","straw-raincoat-cake","ribbon-noodles","jiang-shilang-tofu"]},{key:"transmission",zh:"传承断裂",en:"Broken Transmission",descZh:"依赖特定个人、家族或宫廷的传承中断",descEn:"Transmission tied to one person, family or court was severed",examples:["quxian-braised-eggplant","chef-mao-three-delicacy-soup","frozen-tofu-pork-soup","wine-lees-crab"]},{key:"context",zh:"语境消失",en:"Vanished Context",descZh:"赋予菜品意义的文化情境不再",descEn:"The cultural context that gave the dish meaning is gone",examples:["golden-rice","crispy-huangdu","buddhas-delight","jade-grain-soup"]}],t={zh:"表：六类失传原因与对应菜品（部分菜品同时属于多类）",en:"Table: six categories of culinary loss and their dishes (some dishes belong to several categories)"},s=[{zh:"我叫李子安，是一名高中生。",en:"My name is Jerry Zian Li, and I am a high school student."},{zh:'和很多同龄人一样，我喜欢吃，也喜欢历史。但真正让我着迷的，是一个有点"冷门"的问题：古人到底吃什么？那些写在古书里的菜，今天还在吗？',en:"Like many people my age, I love food and I love history. But what really fascinates me is a slightly unusual question: What did ancient Chinese people actually eat? And are those dishes — the ones written about in old books — still around today?"},{zh:"一开始，我只是好奇。后来查得多了，才发现一个让我心里一震的事实：唐宋元明清那么多菜，绝大多数已经失传了。有的只留下一个名字，有的连做法都没传下来，有的就算有方子，也因为食材、口味、社会的变迁，再也做不出原来的样子。",en:"At first, I was just curious. But the more I looked, the more I discovered something that truly surprised me: of the countless dishes from the Tang, Song, Yuan, Ming, and Qing dynasties, the vast majority have been lost. Some survive only as names. Others have no recorded recipe at all. Even when a recipe survives, the ingredients, tastes, and society have changed so much that the original dish can no longer be recreated exactly."},{zh:"我觉得可惜。不只是可惜味道没了，更是可惜——一道菜里藏着的东西，比我们想的多得多。",en:"I felt that was a shame. Not just because the flavors are gone, but because a dish holds far more than we realize."},{zh:"比如苏轼在黄州煮的那道鱼。他被贬官，生活清贫，却写下一套讲究的煮鱼法：冷水下锅，不搅动，汤面只微微动，用姜、酒、橘皮去腥。这道菜清汤本味，不靠重酱重料。表面上看，是宋代文人喜欢清淡；往深里想，是一个失意的读书人，在逆境里用最朴素的方式，认真对待一顿饭。后来这道菜慢慢没人做了，因为明清以后大家口味越来越重，这种清雅的做法就被挤到了边缘。",en:"Take the fish Su Shi cooked in Huangzhou. He had been exiled and life was hard, yet he wrote a meticulous method for boiling fish: cold water, no stirring, the broth surface barely trembling, using ginger, wine, and orange peel to remove fishiness. The dish is clear and simple, without relying on heavy sauces. On the surface, it reflects the Song literati's taste for lightness. But beneath that, it is a disgraced scholar-official, in adversity, treating a simple meal with utmost seriousness. Later, the dish faded away — because after the Ming and Qing, tastes grew heavier, and this refined, subtle approach was pushed to the margins."},{zh:'再比如清代那些精致菜，像蒋侍郎豆腐、随园豆豉黄鱼、红煨羊肉。它们消失，不只是因为工序麻烦、食材难找，更根本的原因是：做这些菜的人，和吃这些菜的人，在近代社会大变革里散掉了。大户人家没了，私厨流散了，城市里兴起了更热烈、更下饭的"江湖菜"。饮食版图变了，其实是社会结构变了。',en:`Then there are the exquisite dishes of the Qing dynasty — Minister Jiang's Tofu, Suiyuan Yellow Croaker with Fermented Black Beans, Red-Braised Lamb. They disappeared not only because their techniques were demanding or ingredients scarce, but because the people who made them and ate them were scattered by modern social upheaval. Great households collapsed, private chefs dispersed, and a bolder, more rustic "jianghu cuisine" rose in the cities. The culinary map changed — but that was really a change in social structure.`},{zh:"所以我慢慢明白：饮食是一面镜子。它照见的不只是盘子里有什么，还有一个时代的气候、物产、阶层、审美，甚至一个社会的权力结构。我们失去了这些菜，也就失去了一条理解历史的线索。",en:"So I came to understand: food is a mirror. It reflects not only what is on the plate, but the climate, produce, class, aesthetics, and even the power structure of an era. When we lose these dishes, we lose a vital clue to understanding history."},{zh:'这就是我要做"饮食寻根"的原因。',en:"This is why I am searching for culinary roots."},{zh:'我说的"寻根"，不是简单地找回几道菜的做法，而是通过复刻古菜，去追问它们背后的文化脉络：这道菜诞生在什么样的社会里？是哪些人在吃？它代表了什么？它为什么消失？它的消失，又和哪些历史变迁有关？',en:'"Searching for roots," as I mean it, is not simply recovering recipes. It is using the recreation of lost dishes to trace the cultural threads behind them: In what kind of society was this dish born? Who ate it? What did it represent? Why did it disappear? And what historical transformations does its disappearance reflect?'}],o=[{title:{zh:"第一，查。",en:"First, research."},zh:"从《苏轼文集》《山家清供》《随园食单》《宋氏养生部》《饮膳正要》这些古书里找原始记载。同时查历史背景、社会研究，把菜放回它那个时代去理解。遇到只有菜名没有做法的，就去翻同时代的笔记、诗集、考古报告，尽量还原。",en:"I search ancient texts — The Collected Works of Su Shi, Shanjia Qinggong, Suiyuan Shidan, Song Family Dietary Methods, Principles of Correct Diet — for original records. I also look into historical background and social studies to place the dish in its era. When only a name survives, I dig into notes, poems, and archaeological reports from the same period to reconstruct as much as possible."},{title:{zh:"第二，做。",en:"Second, cook."},zh:'在自家厨房里亲手试。按古方一步步来，用手机拍下每一个步骤。有些菜要试很多次，比如东坡黄州煮鱼要求"汤面只允许微微微动"，火候全凭经验，失败是常事。但每次失败，都让我更懂古人的讲究。',en:`I make the dish in my own kitchen, following the ancient recipe step by step, photographing every stage with my phone. Some dishes take many attempts. Dongpo's Huangzhou Boiled Fish requires the broth surface to "barely tremble" — a level of heat control that depends entirely on experience. Failure is normal. But every failure deepens my understanding of ancient culinary wisdom.`},{title:{zh:"第三，写。",en:"Third, write."},zh:'每做完一道菜，写一份中英双语的"菜品档案"：它出自哪本书、原文怎么说、它诞生在什么社会背景里、代表了什么文化、为什么失传、和哪些历史变迁有关、我复刻时做了哪些调整、成品是什么色香味形。英文不是机翻，是写给外国读者看的文化注释，让他们明白中国饮食背后的社会逻辑。',en:'After each dish, I write a bilingual "dish archive": which book it comes from, what the original text says, what social background it emerged from, what culture it represents, why it was lost, what historical shifts its disappearance reflects, what adjustments I made, and what the finished dish looks, smells, and tastes like. The English is not machine-translated; it is a cultural note for international readers, explaining the social logic behind Chinese food.'},{title:{zh:"第四，分享。",en:"Fourth, share."},zh:'把这些内容做成一个中英双语网站。每道菜有独立页面、照片、文献出处。它既是菜谱，也是研究档案，更是一份"饮食寻根"的记录。',en:'I put all of this into a bilingual website. Each dish has its own page, photos, and references. It is at once a recipe collection, a research archive, and a record of "culinary root-searching."'}],r=[{zh:'饮食文化的交流。让外国人看到的不只是"中国菜好吃"，而是"中国古人在一千年前就这样思考食物与社会、与生活的关系"。也让国内的年轻人重新发现，我们的祖先留下的不只是"八大菜系"，还有无数承载着历史记忆和文化密码的味道。',en:'Cultural exchange through food. I want international visitors to see not just "Chinese food is delicious," but "this is how Chinese people a thousand years ago thought about food, society, and life." I also hope young people in China will rediscover that our ancestors left us not only the "Eight Great Cuisines," but countless flavors carrying historical memory and cultural codes.'},{zh:'建立一份"失传古菜研究档案"。我的复刻不是完美还原，很多地方只能推测和调整。但我会把每一次尝试、每一处存疑、每一条参考文献都如实记下来。这份档案是一个起点，让后来的人可以接着做，让这些菜不至于彻底消失。',en:"To build a research archive of lost ancient dishes. My recreations are not perfect. Many are inferences and adjustments. But I record every attempt, every uncertainty, and every reference honestly. This archive is a starting point — so others can continue the work, and these dishes will not disappear completely."},{zh:"用饮食讲述历史。我相信，通过一道道菜的故事，可以让更多人理解：历史不只是帝王将相的年表，也是普通人餐桌上的变迁；社会变革不只是教科书上的名词，也体现在一道菜的消失与重生之中。饮食的寻根，最终是对我们自身文化身份的追问。",en:"To tell history through food. I believe that through the stories of individual dishes, more people can understand: history is not just chronologies of emperors and generals, but changes on ordinary people's tables. Social transformation is not just a term in textbooks — it is embodied in the disappearance and revival of a dish. Culinary root-searching is, ultimately, a quest for our own cultural identity."}],h=[{zh:"我是一名高中生，能做的不多。但我想用这种方式，为中国的饮食文化做一点微小而长久的事——不只是记住味道，更是记住味道背后的历史。",en:"I am a high school student. I cannot do much. But I want to do this small, lasting thing for Chinese food culture — not just to remember flavors, but to remember the history behind them."},{zh:"如果你也对古代饮食感兴趣，欢迎和我交流。也许下一道被复活的菜，就来自你的灵感。",en:"If you are also interested in ancient cuisine, I welcome you to reach out. Maybe the next dish to be revived will come from your idea."}];export{n as a,a as b,t as c,s as d,o as e,r as f,h as g,i as l,e as n};
